{
  "stage": "write",
  "item": "ch02.s04",
  "inputs_hash": "ae31851c15899335c8623cf84304da9f415634c08ef52b479611bd9455c4d26c",
  "output_hash": "8e2218019dc59d18dc5ec07a6237130d7852b822cb89f57f11b77b2dae2a2601",
  "output": {
    "section_id": "ch02.s04",
    "paragraphs": [
      {
        "id": "ch02.s04.p01",
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "ch02.s04.p01.01",
            "text": "Why does a simple word tokenizer break down once it meets real user input?",
            "type": "C",
            "cites": []
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s04.p01.02",
            "text": "A main problem with word-based tokenization is handling words that are not present in the vocabulary.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "fKd8s29e-l4@250",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@252",
              "mk-6cFebjis@1793",
              "mk-6cFebjis@1798"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s04.p01.03",
            "text": "These words are called out-of-vocabulary words.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "fKd8s29e-l4@272",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@274",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@277"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s04.p01.04",
            "text": "A vocabulary here is a dictionary with a collection of tokens arranged in ascending order, where every token corresponds to a token ID.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "fKd8s29e-l4@328",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@330",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@333",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@335"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s04.p01.05",
            "text": "If a user inputs a word that was not present in that vocabulary, a word-based tokenization scheme can run into an error.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "fKd8s29e-l4@268",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@270",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@272",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@307",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@310"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s04.p01.06",
            "text": "For example, if the available data contains only a small case such as \"my hobby is playing Cricket\" and a new word such as \"football\" is not present in that data set, these schemes usually run into an error.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "fKd8s29e-l4@294",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@296",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@298",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@300",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@303",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@306",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@307",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@310"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s04.p01.07",
            "text": "The same issue appears when trying to encode text containing words such as \"hello\" that are not in the vocabulary: the tokenizer returns an error because it does not know how to encode them.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "mk-6cFebjis@1297",
              "mk-6cFebjis@1300",
              "mk-6cFebjis@1302",
              "mk-6cFebjis@1305",
              "mk-6cFebjis@1314",
              "mk-6cFebjis@1316",
              "mk-6cFebjis@1318",
              "mk-6cFebjis@1321",
              "mk-6cFebjis@1330",
              "mk-6cFebjis@1333",
              "mk-6cFebjis@1338",
              "mk-6cFebjis@1340",
              "mk-6cFebjis@1342",
              "mk-6cFebjis@1344",
              "mk-6cFebjis@1346"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s04.p01.08",
            "text": "Out-of-vocabulary words are like preparing for an exam and then being asked a completely different question in the exam.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "fKd8s29e-l4@279",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@282",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@284",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@285"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "ch02.s04.p02",
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "ch02.s04.p02.01",
            "text": "Word tokenization also misses an important kind of structure inside related words.",
            "type": "C",
            "cites": []
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s04.p02.02",
            "text": "Another major problem with word-based tokenization is that similar words such as \"boy\" and \"boys\" are treated as separate words despite their similarity.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "fKd8s29e-l4@341",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@344",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@361",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@366",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@375",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@377",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@379",
              "mk-6cFebjis@1805",
              "mk-6cFebjis@1808",
              "mk-6cFebjis@1810",
              "mk-6cFebjis@1812",
              "mk-6cFebjis@1814"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s04.p02.03",
            "text": "In a word-based scheme, different forms of related words get separate token IDs, so similarity based on a shared root word is not captured.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "fKd8s29e-l4@348",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@351",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@366",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@370",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@372",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@377",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@379",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@382",
              "mk-6cFebjis@1814",
              "mk-6cFebjis@1816",
              "mk-6cFebjis@1821",
              "mk-6cFebjis@1823",
              "mk-6cFebjis@1825",
              "mk-6cFebjis@1827",
              "mk-6cFebjis@1829",
              "mk-6cFebjis@1832",
              "mk-6cFebjis@1835",
              "mk-6cFebjis@1839"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s04.p02.04",
            "text": "The token IDs for such words may be far apart, and in either case the tokenization itself does not capture that they are very similar.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "fKd8s29e-l4@351",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@353",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@356",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@358",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@361",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@364",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@366"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "ch02.s04.p03",
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "ch02.s04.p03.01",
            "text": "There is a trade-off here.",
            "type": "C",
            "cites": []
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s04.p03.02",
            "text": "Word-based tokenization produces shorter tokenized sequences because each word is one token.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "fKd8s29e-l4@627",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@639",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@643",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@645",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@648",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@654",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@657"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s04.p03.03",
            "text": "For example, \"dinosaur\" is just one token in word-based tokenization.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "fKd8s29e-l4@595",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@598",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@645",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@648"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s04.p03.04",
            "text": "Its disadvantages are that it fails on out-of-vocabulary words and that the vocabulary size is pretty large.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "fKd8s29e-l4@630",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@633",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@634",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@637"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s04.p03.05",
            "text": "A major reason for the large vocabulary is that a model would need tokens for a huge number of words in the language.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "mk-6cFebjis@1843",
              "mk-6cFebjis@1845",
              "mk-6cFebjis@1847",
              "mk-6cFebjis@1849",
              "mk-6cFebjis@1856",
              "mk-6cFebjis@1858",
              "mk-6cFebjis@1860"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s04.p03.06",
            "text": "The number of words in English is estimated to be around 600,000 to 1 million.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "mk-6cFebjis@1860",
              "mk-6cFebjis@1862",
              "mk-6cFebjis@1866",
              "mk-6cFebjis@1868",
              "mk-6cFebjis@1870",
              "mk-6cFebjis@1872"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s04.p03.07",
            "text": "That makes the required vocabulary size really very high for a word-based tokenizer.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "mk-6cFebjis@1872",
              "mk-6cFebjis@1874",
              "mk-6cFebjis@1878",
              "mk-6cFebjis@1881",
              "mk-6cFebjis@1883",
              "mk-6cFebjis@1885",
              "mk-6cFebjis@1888"
            ]
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
